GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts\lunaabsentcpu\fable_p1.md:35:**F3. THE SIO LINE-5 ROUTE IS SYNCHRONOUS AND GUEST-DRIVABLE WHILE STEPPING (kimi UPHELD, verified).** Anchor `case OBIO_SIO + 4:` (cmd channel 0, 0x51000004): first write with `obio_sio_regno == 0` selects `regnr = idata & 7` (store 0x01 -> WR1); second write lands `d->obio_sio_wr[sio_devnr][regnr] = idata`; then `if (d->obio_sio_wr[sio_devnr][SCC_WR1] & SCC_WR1_TX_IE && !old_tx_enable) d->sio_tx_pending[sio_devnr] = 1;` -> `reassert_serial_interrupt(d)` -> `INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->sio_irq)` -> `luna88k_interrupt_assert` (connected to `%s.luna88k.5`, `templ.line = 5`) -> `interrupt_status |= 1 << 30` -> `reassert_interrupts(d)`. `SCC_WR1_TX_IE` = 0x02 (`src/include/thirdparty/sccreg.h`). Two word stores, no host timer. **Disarm is also synchronous**: a cmd write of 0x28 (`SCC_RESET_TX_IP`) -> `sio_tx_pending = 0` -> `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d->sio_irq)` -> bit 30 cleared -> `reassert_interrupts` — which, under an ASSERT-only NULL check, is a DEASSERT of NULL: the disarm doubles as the M_NULLCHECK row. Session-order caveat: bit 30 stays pending machine-wide until disarmed, so place the arming after W1 or disarm at once, or the pre-fix session dies on an arming store instead of a SOFT store.
